# Knowledge tasks (50)
knowledge_tasks = [
    msg("What is the difference between TCP and UDP?", "TCP is connection-oriented, guarantees delivery order, and retransmits lost packets. UDP is connectionless, faster, but does not guarantee delivery. TCP is used for web pages and email. UDP is used for streaming and gaming."),
    msg("Explain how a hash table works.", "A hash table maps keys to values using a hash function. The function converts a key to an array index. Collisions (when two keys map to the same index) are handled by chaining (linked lists at each index) or open addressing (probing for the next empty slot). Average lookup is O(1)."),
    msg("What is the CAP theorem?", "The CAP theorem states that a distributed system can provide at most two of three guarantees: Consistency (all nodes see the same data), Availability (every request gets a response), and Partition tolerance (the system works despite network failures). In practice, partition tolerance is mandatory, so the choice is between consistency and availability."),
    msg("Explain the difference between SQL and NoSQL databases.", "SQL databases are relational, use structured schemas, support ACID transactions, and use SQL for queries. NoSQL databases are non-relational, support flexible schemas, scale horizontally, and come in types like document (MongoDB), key-value (Redis), columnar (Cassandra), and graph (Neo4j)."),
    msg("What is Big O notation?", "Big O describes the upper bound of an algorithm's growth rate as input size increases. O(1) is constant, O(log n) is logarithmic, O(n) is linear, O(n log n) is linearithmic, O(n^2) is quadratic. It helps compare algorithm efficiency independently of hardware."),
    msg("Explain what REST means in API design.", "REST (Representational State Transfer) is an architectural style using HTTP methods (GET, POST, PUT, DELETE) to interact with resources identified by URLs. It is stateless - each request contains all information needed. Resources are represented in formats like JSON or XML."),
    msg("What is the difference between a process and a thread?", "A process is an independent program execution with its own memory space. A thread is a lightweight unit of execution within a process, sharing the process's memory. Threads are faster to create and switch between, but shared memory requires synchronisation to avoid race conditions."),
    msg("Explain how HTTPS works.", "HTTPS uses TLS (Transport Layer Security) on top of HTTP. The browser and server perform a handshake: the server sends its certificate, the browser verifies it against trusted CAs, they agree on encryption keys using asymmetric cryptography, then communicate using symmetric encryption. This ensures confidentiality and integrity."),
    msg("What is recursion?", "Recursion is when a function calls itself to solve a problem by breaking it into smaller subproblems. It requires a base case (stopping condition) and a recursive case. Example: factorial(n) = n * factorial(n-1), with base case factorial(0) = 1. It uses the call stack, which can overflow for deep recursion."),
    msg("Explain what Docker containers are.", "Docker containers package an application with all its dependencies into a standardised unit. Unlike virtual machines, containers share the host OS kernel, making them lightweight and fast to start. Containers are built from images defined in Dockerfiles and can be orchestrated using tools like Kubernetes."),
    msg("What is the difference between compiled and interpreted languages?", "Compiled languages (C, Rust) are translated to machine code before execution, producing fast executables. Interpreted languages (Python, JavaScript) are executed line by line by an interpreter, offering flexibility but typically slower performance. Some languages (Java) use both: compiled to bytecode, then interpreted by a VM."),
    msg("Explain what a blockchain is.", "A blockchain is a distributed, immutable ledger of transactions. Each block contains a hash of the previous block, a timestamp, and transaction data. This chain structure makes tampering evident. Consensus mechanisms (Proof of Work, Proof of Stake) ensure agreement among participants without a central authority."),
    msg("What is machine learning?", "Machine learning is a subset of AI where systems learn patterns from data without explicit programming. Supervised learning uses labelled data to predict outcomes. Unsupervised learning finds patterns in unlabelled data. Reinforcement learning learns through trial and error with rewards. Common algorithms include linear regression, decision trees, and neural networks."),
    msg("Explain the MVC design pattern.", "MVC separates an application into three components: Model (data and business logic), View (user interface), and Controller (handles user input and updates the model). This separation makes code more maintainable and testable. The user interacts with the View, which sends events to the Controller, which updates the Model, which notifies the View to refresh."),
    msg("What is GDPR?", "The General Data Protection Regulation is an EU law governing personal data processing. Key principles: lawfulness, purpose limitation, data minimisation, accuracy, storage limitation, integrity, and accountability. It gives individuals rights including access, erasure, portability, and objection. Non-compliance can result in fines up to 4% of annual global turnover."),
]

